= Solutions for Surgery Paper-II (Ophthalmology & Otolaryngology)
== Session: May-2022

=== GROUP-A (Ophthalmology)

*1. a. Draw and label the anatomy of lacrimal apparatus.*

- Lacrimal gland
- Lacrimal puncta
- Lacrimal canaliculi
- Lacrimal sac
- Nasolacrimal duct

*b. Write the layers of pre-corneal tear film with their functions.*

- *Lipid layer (outer layer):* It is secreted by the Meibomian glands. Functions: (i) To prevent evaporation of the aqueous layer. (ii) It increases the surface tension & prevents the over-flow of tear.
- *Aqueous layer (middle layer):* It is secreted by the lacrimal gland & accessory lacrimal glands. Functions: (i) Provides atmospheric oxygen to the corneal epithelium. (ii) Antibacterial function. (iii) To wash away the debris & other noxious materials. (iv) It gives nutrition to corneal epithelium.
- *Mucin layer (inner layer):* It is secreted by the conjunctival goblet cells. Functions: (i) To converts the corneal epithelium from a hydrophobic to a hydrophilic surface. (ii) To wet the microvilli of the corneal epithelium & thereby retains the pre-corneal tear film.

*c. How will you manage a case of acute dacryocystitis?*

- *During cellulitis stage:* (i) Hot fomentation. (ii) Systemic & topical antibiotics. (iii) Systemic analgesics.
- *During stage of lacrimal abscess:* (i) Hot fomentation. (ii) Systemic & topical antibiotics. (iii) Systemic analgesics. (iv) Drained by small incision. (v) When acute infection subsides, dacryocystectomy (DCT) or dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) operation.
- *Treatment of external lacrimal fistula:* After controlling the acute infection, fistulectomy with DCT or DCR operation.

*2. a. What is the most likely diagnosis?*
- Posterior capsular opacification (PCO).

*b. Mention the differential diagnosis.*
- (i) Pseudophakia. (ii) Pseudoexfoliation.

*c. How will you treat the patient?*
- Nd-YAG- laser capsulotomy.

*3. a. Define and classify keratoplasty.*

- *Definition:* Keratoplasty, also called corneal grafting or corneal transplantation, is an operation in which the patient's diseased cornea is replaced by the healthy cornea.
- *Classification:* 
(
  1) Autokeratoplasty: (a) Rotational keratoplasty. (b) Contralateral keratoplasty. 
(
  2) Allografting or allokeratoplasty: (a) Penetrating keratoplasty. (b) Lamellar keratoplasty (Anterior/Posterior). (c) Small patch graft.

*b. What are the indications of keratoplasty?*

- *Optical keratoplasty:* 
(
  1) Keratoconus. 
(
  2) Scarring. 
(
  3) Corneal dystrophies. 
(
  4) Pseudophakic bullous keratopathy. 
(
  5) Corneal degenerations.
- *Tectonic grafting:* Thinning with descemetocele.
- *Therapeutic:* Eyes unresponsive to antimicrobial therapy.
- *Cosmetic:* To improve the appearance of the eye.

*c. What are the contraindications to ocular tissue donation?*

- *Medical:* 
(
  1) AIDS. 
(
  2) HIV seropositivity. 
(
  3) Rabies. 
(
  4) Active hepatitis B and hepatitis C. 
(
  5) Septicaemia. 
(
  6) Creutzfeldt Jacob disease. 
(
  7) Leukaemia. 
(
  8) Syphilis.
- *Ophthalmic:* 
(
  1) Previous intraocular surgery. 
(
  2) Corneal pathology. 
(
  3) Retinoblastoma. 
(
  4) Malignant melanoma. 
(
  5) Iridocyclitis. 
(
  6) Endophthalmitis.

*4. a. What is your diagnosis?*
- Chalazion.

*b. What is the pathogenesis of this lesion?*

Chronic inflammation of the gland by a low virulence organism -> Blockage of the duct of the Meibomian gland -> Accumulation of lipid secretion -> Breakdown of lipids into components of oleic acid -> Mucosal irritation -> Cellular infiltration with formation of giant cells -> Proliferation of fibroblasts -> Granuloma formation.

*c. What is the treatment?*

Incision & through curettage under local anaesthesia is done as an OPD procedure.

*5. a. What is ocular hypertension?*

The term ocular hypertension is labelled when a patient has an IOP constantly more than
21 mm of Hg but no optic disc & visual field changes.

*b. What are the signs of primary open angle glaucoma?*

(i) Raised intraocular pressure (IOP) with diurnal variations. (ii) Visual acuity may remain good till the late stage. (iii) Cornea is usually clear. (iv) Anterior chamber depth is normal. (v) Pupillary reaction remains normal until the late stage. (vi) Cupping of the optic disc. (vii) Change in the visual field.

*c. Write down the treatment of primary open angle glaucoma.*

- *Medical therapy:* (Topical) B-Blockers (Timolol maleate), Prostaglandin analogues (Latanoprost), Alpha agonists (Brimonidine), Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (Dorzolamide), Miotics (Pilocarpine). (Systemic) Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide).
- *Laser therapy:* Argon laser trabeculoplasty (ALT).
- *Surgical therapy:* 
(
  1) Trabeculectomy. 
(
  2) Newer modifications (Shunt/valve).

*6. Write short notes on: a. Ptosis, b. Mydriatics.*
- *Ptosis:*  Abnormally low position or drooping of the upper eyelid below its normal position is called ptosis. Ptosis may be unilateral or bilateral and partial or complete. Causes include neurogenic, myogenic, aponeurotic, and mechanical.
- *Mydriatics:*  Mydriatics are the drugs which cause pupillary dilatation. Types: 
(
  1) Parasympatholytic mydriatics (Atropine, Tropicamide). 
(
  2) Sympathomimetic mydriatics (Phenylephrine, Cocaine). Indications include fundus examination, pre-operative assessment of cataract, and breaking posterior synechiae.

=== GROUP-B (Ophthalmology)

*1. a. What is your diagnosis?*
- Orbital cellulitis.

*b. How will you treat the boy?*

(i) Immediate hospital admission. (ii) Intensive antibiotic therapy. (iii) Analgesics & anti-inflammatory drugs. (iv) Topical antibiotic eye ointment. (v) Nasal decongestant drops. (vi) Revaluation. (vii) Surgical intervention (Drainage of orbital/lid abscess, drainage of infected sinuses).

*c. What complications may occur if remains untreated?*

(i) Exposure keratopathy. (ii) Optic neuritis. (iii) Central retinal artery occlusion. (iv) Subperiosteal abscess. (v) Orbital abscess. (vi) Temporal or parotid abscess. (vii) Cavernous sinus thrombosis. (viii) Meningitis. (ix) Brain abscess. (x) General septicaemia or pyaemia.

*2. a. Define and classify ametropia?*

- *Definition:* Ametropia can be defined as a state of refraction, when the parallel rays of light coming from infinity (with accommodation being at rest) are focused either in front or behind the sensitive layer of retina, in one or both meridians.
- *Classification:* 
(
  1) Myopia. 
(
  2) Hypermetropia. 
(
  3) Astigmatism.

*b. Write down the procedure of measuring visual acuity both distance and near.*

- *For distance vision:* Snellen chart is mostly used. The patient is placed at a distance of 6 metres.
- *For near vision:* Jaeger's chart or Roman test type is used. The patient holds the chart at normal reading distance.

*c. What is astigmatism?*

Astigmatism is a type of refractive error wherein the rays of light entering the eye cannot converge to a point focus but form focal lines.

*3. a. What is your diagnosis?*
- Optic neuritis.

*b. What other ocular features you may find in this patient?*

(i) Decreased visual acuity. (ii) Local tenderness. (iii) Pupillary reaction is sluggished & relative afferent pupillary defect. (iv) Impaired colour vision (dyschromatopsia). (v) Delayed dark adaptation. (vi) Field of vision - central, centro-caecal or para central scotoma.

*c. How will you treat the patient?*

(i) Treatment must be directed to the cause. (ii) Corticosteroid IV methyl prednisolone for 3 consecutive days, followed by oral prednisolone. (iii) ACTH injection. (iv) Injection of Vitamin B1, B6 & B12.

*5. a. Draw and label the layers of retina.*

1. Internal limiting membrane.
2. Nerve fibre layer.
3. Ganglion cell layer.
4. Inner plexiform layer.
5. Inner nuclear layer.
6. Outer plexiform layer.
7. Outer nuclear layer.
8. External limiting membrane.
9. Layer of rods & cones. 10. Pigment epithelium.

*b. What do you mean by Argyll Robertson pupil?*

It is a bilateral abnormality characterized by failure of the pupils to constrict with light, but retention of constriction presents with accommodation.

*c. Write down the clinical features of sixth nerve palsy.*

(i) The eyeball is rotated inward (convergent squint). (ii) Defective abduction of the eye, partially or completely. (iii) Face turn toward the field of action of paralyzed muscle.

*6. Write short notes on: a. Ocular chemical injury, b. LASIK.*
- *Ocular chemical injury:*  Categorized into alkali and acid burns. Grading (Hughes and Roper-Hall) ranges from Grade-I (good prognosis) to Grade-IV (poor prognosis). Management involves immediate vigorous washing with normal saline/water for
30 minutes, followed by medical (steroids, atropine, antibiotics) and potentially surgical treatment (limbal stem cell transplantation).
- *LASIK:*  Means Laser Assisted Stromal In situ Keratomileusis. A superficial corneal flap is lifted, and the central corneal stromal bed is ablated by excimer laser. Used to correct myopia, hypermetropia, and astigmatism.

=== GROUP-C (Otolaryngology)

*1. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Otosclerosis.

*b. What are the clinical interpretations of Tuning Fork Test?*

- *Rinne test:* Negative (BC > AC).
- *Weber test:* Lateralized to the diseased (poorer) ear.
- *ABC test:* Normal.

*c. What are treatment modalities? Write down five causes of conductive type of deafness.*
- *Treatment modalities:* (i) Surgical: Stapedectomy/stapedotomy with placement of prosthesis. (ii) Hearing Aid.
- *Five causes of conductive deafness:*  
(
  1) Congenital: Meatal atresia. 
(
  2) External ear: Wax, Foreign body. 
(
  3) Middle ear: Perforation of TM, Ossicular discontinuity.

*2. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Enlarged adenoid.

*b. What are the investigations for diagnosis?*

(i) X-ray of the nasopharynx lateral view. (ii) X-ray of the paranasal sinuses occipito-mental view. (iii) Nasopharyngoscopy.

*c. How do you treat this child?*

Adenoidectomy under general anaesthesia with the help of adenoid curette.

*3. a. What is the clinical diagnosis?*
- Allergic rhinitis.

*b. What are the causes of nasal obstruction?*

(i) Nasal polyp. (ii) DNS. (iii) Acute & chronic rhinitis. (iv) Atrophic rhinitis. (v) Foreign body.

*c. How do you treat this patient?*

(i) Avoidance of allergen. (ii) Antihistamines. (iii) Topical or oral steroids. (iv) Nasal decongestants. (v) Immunotherapy.

*4. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Achalasia cardia.

*b. How will you investigate & treat the patient?*

- *Investigations:* (i) Barium swallow X-ray (Rat tail appearance). (ii) Oesophagoscopy. (iii) Oesophageal manometry.
- *Treatment:* (i) Balloon dilatation. (ii) Heller’s cardiomyotomy. (iii) Botulinum toxin injection.

*c. Enumerate five oesophageal causes of dysphagia.*


(
  1) Achalasia cardia. 
(
  2) Carcinoma oesophagus. 
(
  3) Oesophageal stricture. 
(
  4) Foreign body in oesophagus. 
(
  5) Plummer Vinson syndrome.

*5. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Acute follicular tonsillitis.

*b. How will you treat the boy?*

(i) Bed rest. (ii) Plenty of fluids. (iii) Systemic antibiotics (Penicillin). (iv) Analgesics. (v) Warm saline gargles.

*c. Enumerate five causes of white patches over tonsils.*


(
  1) Acute follicular tonsillitis. 
(
  2) Diphtheria. 
(
  3) Vincent’s angina. 
(
  4) Infectious mononucleosis. 
(
  5) Oral thrush (Moniliasis).

*6. Write short notes on: i) FESS, ii) Ludwig’s Angina.*
- *FESS:*  Functional endoscopic sinus surgery. A minimally invasive technique using rigid endoscopes to restore sinus ventilation and function. Indications: chronic sinusitis, polyps, source of epistaxis.
- *Ludwig’s Angina:*  Virulent sepsis of the sublingual and submandibular spaces. Usually dental in origin. Features include brawny swelling, trismus, and odynophagia. Management: high dose antibiotics, incision and drainage.

=== GROUP-D (Otolaryngology)

*1. a. What are the causes of voice change?*


(
  1) Acute/Chronic laryngitis. 
(
  2) Carcinoma larynx. 
(
  3) Vocal nodule. 
(
  4) Vocal cord polyp. 
(
  5) Laryngeal papilloma.

*b. How will you investigate?*

(i) Indirect laryngoscopy. (ii) Direct laryngoscopy. (iii) Biopsy and histopathology. (iv) CT scan/MRI.

*c. How will you treat the patient?*
- Depends on cause. If Ca larynx: Radiotherapy, Surgery (Total laryngectomy), or Chemotherapy.

*2. a. What is your most probable diagnosis?*
- Nasopharyngeal carcinoma.

*b. How will you investigate & treat this patient?*

- *Investigation:* (i) Posterior rhinoscopy. (ii) Punch biopsy. (iii) FNAC from neck nodes. (iv) CT scan/MRI.
- *Treatment:* (i) Radiotherapy (main modality). (ii) Radical block dissection of neck nodes. (iii) Chemotherapy.

*c. Enumerate 5 causes of epistaxis in adult.*


(
  1) Trauma. 
(
  2) Hypertension. 
(
  3) Malignancy (nasal/nasopharyngeal). 
(
  4) Blood dyscrasias. 
(
  5) Idiopathic (from Little’s area).

*3. a. What are the causes of thyroid enlargement?*


(
  1) Physiological (puberty, pregnancy). 
(
  2) Iodine deficiency (Endemic goitre). 
(
  3) Dyshormonogenesis. 
(
  4) Goitrogens in diet. 
(
  5) Neoplasms.

*b. How will you investigate this case?*

(i) Thyroid function tests (T3, T4, TSH). (ii) USG of neck. (iii) FNAC of the swelling. (iv) Radioisotope scan.

*c. Write down the complications of thyroid surgery.*

- *Intraoperative:* Haemorrhage, Injury to RLN or SLN.
- *Immediate:* Haematoma, Laryngeal oedema, Respiratory obstruction.
- *Late:* Hypothyroidism, Hypocalcaemia (Tetany), Thyroid storm.

*4. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Attico-antral variety of CSOM (Unsafe variety).

*b. How will you investigate & treat the boy?*

- *Investigation:* (i) Examination under microscope. (ii) PTA & audiogram. (iii) X-ray mastoid Town's view. (iv) CT scan.
- *Treatment:* Surgical (Mastoidectomy - radical or modified radical).

*c. Write down the complications of surgical procedure.*


(
  1) Injury to facial nerve. 
(
  2) Dislocation of incus. 
(
  3) Injury to sigmoid sinus. 
(
  4) Injury to dura. 
(
  5) Labyrinthitis.

*5. a. What is your clinical diagnosis?*
- Tubercular lymphadenitis.

*b. How will you investigate this case?*

(i) Blood for TC, DC, ESR. (ii) Mantoux test. (iii) FNAC (AFB stain). (iv) Lymph node biopsy (Histopathology). (v) Chest X-ray.

*c. What are the causes of cervical lymph nodes enlargement?*


(
  1) Infections (Acute/Chronic e.g. TB). 
(
  2) Neoplasms (Primary e.g. Lymphoma; Secondary e.g. Metastatic). 
(
  3) Connective tissue disorders.

*6. Write short notes on: i) Tracheostomy, ii) Acute retropharyngeal abscess.*
- *Tracheostomy:*  Artificial opening in the anterior wall of the trachea. Used to relieve respiratory obstruction or retain secretions. Complications include haemorrhage, apnoea, surgical emphysema.
- *Acute retropharyngeal abscess:*  Suppuration of retropharyngeal lymph nodes. Common in children. Features: fever, dysphagia, widening of prevertebral space on lateral X-ray. Treatment: incision and drainage, antibiotics.





