5. Endothelium.

*c) How do you differentiate Fungal corneal ulcer from Bacterial corneal ulcer?*

- *Bacterial corneal ulcer:* Excavated, moist, centrally placed lesion; Whole cornea hazy; Hypopyon is thin, with a definite level, and changes with posture.
- *Fungal corneal ulcer:* Elevated, dry, satellite lesion; Multiple satellite projections; Hypopyon is usually thick, dome-shaped, and does not change with posture.

*6. a) Phacolytic glaucoma:*
 Phacolytic glaucoma is a secondary open angle occurring in association with a hypermature cataract. It is caused by the leakage of lens proteins through an intact capsule, which clog the trabecular meshwork.

*b) VKC:*
 Vernal conjunctivitis (Spring catarrh) is a bilateral, recurrent, interstitial, self-limiting, allergic inflammation of the conjunctiva having a periodic seasonal incidence. It typically affects young boys in summer.

