 Optic neuritis is an inflammatory or demyelinating disorder of the optic nerve.

*b) What are the types of optic neuritis?*

1. Papillitis.
2. Neuroretinitis.
3. Retrobulbar neuritis.

*c) Differentiate between papilledema and papillitis.*

- *Vision:* Normal in papilledema; Impaired in papillitis.
- *Pain:* Absent in papilledema; Present on eye movement in papillitis.
- *Pupillary reaction:* Normal in papilledema; Sluggish/Relative afferent pupillary defect in papillitis.

*5. a) What are the causes of leukocoria?*

1. Congenital cataract.
2. Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous.
3. Retinopathy of prematurity.
4. Coats disease.
5. Toxocariasis.
6. Retinoblastoma.

*b) What are the factors responsible for Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP)?*

It occurs in premature infants (less than 1500 gm birth weight), who have been given high concentration of oxygen during the first
10 days of life.

*c) What is the treatment of congenital cataract and what complication will be occur if treatment is delayed?*
- *Treatment:* Surgical removal (Lens aspiration or lensectomy) preferably within 6 weeks of birth for bilateral dense cataracts.
- *Complications:* Stimulus deprivation amblyopia, nystagmus, and squint.

*6. a) Endophthalmitis:*
 Purulent inflammation of the intra-ocular cavities (anterior & posterior chambers and vitreous), usually following intraocular surgery or penetrating injury. Symptoms include severe pain and marked loss of vision.

*b) 3rd nerve palsy:*
 Clinical features include ptosis, eyeball rotated outwards and downwards (divergent squint), ocular movements restricted except outwards, and a dilated, non-reacting pupil.

